# Paths the loop may propose changes to but must not merge.
#
# CHARTER.md rule 13a reserves the enforcement mechanism itself -- not the
# charter or the prompts, which rule 13's 22 August 2026 delegation put back
# in the loop's hands, the same as the rest of this repository. This file
# used to list CHARTER.md and prompts/ too, from when rule 13 called both
# human-owned outright; narrowed to match rule 13a's actual surface the same
# round that rewrote rule 13, so this list does not go on asserting an
# ownership boundary the charter no longer draws.
#
# THIS FILE DOES NOT ENFORCE ANYTHING, and the comment here used to claim it
# did: "with branch protection requiring code-owner review on these paths,
# a pull request touching any of them will not auto-merge however green its
# checks are." That was conditional on a setting that was never made. Read
# from the GitHub API on 2026-08-11, branch protection on main has
# `require_code_owner_reviews: true` but `required_approving_review_count: 0`
# -- and with no approval required, there is nothing for the code-owner rule
# to demand. `enforce_admins` is false as a second, independent hole, and the
# loop pushes as the repository owner.
#
# The consequence was not hypothetical: PR #16 changed a workflow file in
# this directory and merged with zero reviews on 2026-08-11.
#
# What actually holds these paths is the `human-owned-paths` job in
# workflows/pr-checks.yml, which fails on any pull request touching them so
# that auto-merge cannot land one -- and that job only bites once it is added
# to branch protection's required checks, because auto-merge waits on
# required checks alone. Two more jobs in the same workflow,
# `rule-13a-text` and `stop-mechanism`, are not path-based and so have no
# entry here: they fail on a change to specific *content* (rule 13a's own
# text; a diff that clears an active docket/HOLD.md) regardless of what
# paths a pull request otherwise touches.
#
# The ownership below is still worth stating: it routes review requests and
# says who owns these paths. It is documentation, not a gate.
#
# This file is itself covered by the /.github/ rule below, so the loop
# cannot remove its own constraints by editing this list.
#
# check-track-scope.mjs is not a rule 13a path by name, but the same
# reasoning: it holds the map of which files each track may write, and CI
# runs the branch's copy of it -- so a round can grant itself a path and use
# it in the same pull request, which round 78 did. Guarded here and in the
# workflow so a scope change costs a human merge. Rule 11's remedy in
# mechanical form: propose the widening, let someone else decide it.
#
# The three checking scripts joined 2026-08-22, same round as rule 13a
# itself, for the identical reason as check-track-scope.mjs: each is the
# enforcement logic behind a rule 13a reservation (its own text;
# docket/HOLD.md not being cleared in a pull request's diff; the behavioural
# guard that actually runs scripts/orchestrate.sh), and none of the three
# is a workflow file, so none was otherwise reserved -- a pull request could
# have weakened or gutted any of them in the same diff that violates what it
# checks, and every job in .github/ would still report green, since a job
# definition there only says which script to run, not what the script does.
# Not gated: scripts/check-routes.sh, which wires
# scripts/test-orchestrate-hold.mjs into the required `build-and-audit` job.
# Gating it would freeze a file every track edits often for unrelated route
# checks; named as a residual in CHARTER.md's History (2026-08-22) rather
# than fixed by widening this list past the point it stays legible.

/.github/                              @addicted2ai
/scripts/check-track-scope.mjs         @addicted2ai
/scripts/check-13a-unchanged.mjs       @addicted2ai
/scripts/check-hold-mechanism.mjs      @addicted2ai
/scripts/test-orchestrate-hold.mjs     @addicted2ai
